\tag{fdip}
\title{Connections, holonomy and twisted operators}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{If local field frames can differ, how are neighboring values compared?
An ordinary edge difference compares two values in the same field
space. A connection first transports one endpoint value to the other.
The resulting positive precision depends on this extra background data.
Changing frames at the vertices changes its matrix by an isometry;
transport around a cycle retains information that vertex frames cannot
remove.}

\p{A spanning tree reduces this information to finitely many holonomies.
For a complex line on a cycle, the determinant can then be computed
exactly. A nontrivial twist can remove the constant zero mode, and the
normalized complex Gaussian gives an explicit holonomy action on a
supplied circle. Antiperiodic spatial differences provide a related
example: exact kernel dimensions change, while the additional low-energy
branches of the centered symbol remain.}

